/**
 * Stopping a program.
 *
 * These live in the root namespace rather than under a prefix, because a precondition that fails
 * is not a subsystem you opt into - `array<T>::at` and your own code should both be able to say
 * so without an import.
 *
 * Both are compiler builtins: the message and the source location are folded into a constant at
 * the call site, which is the one thing a library function could not do. Nothing links against
 * them, and they have no symbol of their own.
 *
 * The message parameter is spelled `string` because that is what a literal *is*. Nothing reads it
 * at runtime - the text is folded from the token, so the type here only decides what the call site
 * is allowed to write, and a literal has to be able to arrive without a conversion. It also has to
 * stay a **literal**: see the note on each overload.
 */

/**
 * Stops the program with a non-zero exit status.
 *
 * The message goes to stderr, followed by the file and line of the `die` itself. Anything already
 * written to stdout is flushed first, so the two appear in the order they were produced.
 *
 * A `die` ends the block it is in: statements after it are not compiled, and a function whose
 * last statement is a `die` does not also need a `return`.
 */
#[builtin: "die"]
function die() : void;

/**
 * The message must be a **string literal**, not a `string` variable. It is folded into the binary
 * together with the source location, so it has to be readable at compile time - a runtime string
 * has nothing to fold, and is a located error rather than a message that quietly goes missing.
 */
#[builtin: "die"]
function die(string $message) : void;

/**
 * Stops the program when `$condition` is false.
 *
 * Emitted in a debug build only. `echoc build`, and `echoc run --release`, drop the whole call -
 * the condition included, exactly as C's `NDEBUG` drops `assert`. So the condition must be free
 * of side effects the rest of the program depends on.
 *
 * The condition is type-checked either way, so a release build never accepts an assert a debug
 * build would have rejected.
 */
#[builtin: "assert"]
function assert(bool $condition) : void;

/**
 * The message must be a **string literal**, for the reason `die`'s must.
 */
#[builtin: "assert"]
function assert(bool $condition, string $message) : void;
